---
/**
 * foodcraft / WhyBM — "WHY-US" (Tahanan Cafe / BM).
 *
 * ROLE: the argument block, and the ONE tinted ground on the light half of this
 * page. A CENTRED head — the underlined eyebrow over one Libre Caslon Text
 * headline — and beneath it a three-track row of CENTRED columns, each opening
 * with a hairline-ringed DISC holding an ordinal numeral, then the reason's
 * title and the owner's paragraph. No photo, no price, no button: this is the
 * only section in the template that argues rather than describes or lists, and
 * "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html" draws it with nothing else.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * that file's data-component="WHY-US" — nothing is re-derived to survive an
 * admin colour scheme, and no literal hex appears in this file (R1). The colours
 * are the wrapper tokens whose VALUES are the design's hexes:
 *   band ground  #FAF7F3 -> --tint          disc ring #DCCDBE -> --line-3
 *   numeral      #7A4E2D -> --accent        title     #241B14 -> --ink
 *   paragraph    #5C5147 -> --ink-soft
 *   section   background:#FAF7F3; padding:76px 0        (full-bleed)
 *   head      text-align:center; margin-bottom:44px
 *   h2        margin:.26em 0 0; NO max-width (it is centred across the wrap)
 *   grid      repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr)) / gap 38px
 *   disc      60x60; 1px solid #DCCDBE; border-radius:50%; margin:0 auto 18px;
 *             Libre Caslon Text 22px #7A4E2D
 *   title     Libre Caslon Text 400 23px; margin:0 0 10px; #241B14
 *   body      15px / 1.8; #5C5147; margin:0 auto; max-width:34ch
 *
 * THE ROUND DISC IS THE DESIGN'S, NOT A DRIFT. This template is otherwise
 * SHARP — 2px corners, no pill anywhere — so a 50%-radius ring looks at first
 * like a copy-paste from a softer template. It is not: the design draws exactly
 * one circle on the whole page and this is it, a struck medallion around an
 * ordinal, not a pill around a label. Reproduced as handed over. Every other
 * corner in this file is the wrapper's --r.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX: `tcw-` (Tahanan Cafe / why). Deliberately NOT the BL sibling's
 * `why-*` set — an audit had to rename a whole block of classes that arrived
 * verbatim from the template before it. Only the HOUSE STYLE is inherited from
 * WhyBL (this doc block, the guard discipline, the original-vs-display index
 * rule); the palette, the centring, the disc and the metrics are this design's.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the "Why us" group, so the sidebar owns a real input for each — a
 * data-field on an undeclared path is a dead hook that focuses nothing):
 *   why.tag                — the eyebrow. The wrapper's .kick primitive, NOT
 *                            restated here: the underline (border-bottom +
 *                            9px padding) is one of this design's two
 *                            signatures and lives on that primitive, so a
 *                            section that re-declares the eyebrow loses it.
 *   why.headline           — the h2, rendered with set:html so an owner's own
 *                            <em> picks up PageBM's ITALIC WALNUT EMPHASIS (the
 *                            other signature). That rule is GLOBAL in the
 *                            wrapper on purpose: scoped styles cannot reach
 *                            elements created by set:html (R3), so an italic
 *                            rule written in this file would never apply to it,
 *                            and any rule this file ever needs for that <em>
 *                            must be written :global().
 *   why.items.${i}.title   — the reason (h3)
 *   why.items.${i}.body    — the reason's paragraph. CANONICAL key: the value
 *                            can arrive as .description through astro-builder's
 *                            normalizeBlock alias map (line ~897 spells
 *                            { description: 'body' }), and as .desc from older
 *                            payloads, so the READ tolerates all three while the
 *                            data-field always spells the key the schema
 *                            declares — otherwise the sidebar never receives the
 *                            edit.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one visible string is written here.
 * The design's demo copy ("Why here", "Why neighbours keep coming back.") is the
 * DESIGNER filling in their own template; a real cafe gets a page that says only
 * what that cafe said. Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value
 * removes the ELEMENT and not just its text: no eyebrow over nothing, no
 * heading over an empty grid, no empty paragraph, no ringed disc floating above
 * a reason that is not there — and the centred head DIV itself is guarded, so an
 * owner who clears both the tag and the headline does not pay 44px of margin for
 * an empty box.
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored:
 *   · THE NUMERAL. The design's demo data carries an `n` field; our schema
 *     declares no numeral input, so it is computed from the DISPLAY index,
 *     zero-padded to two digits, and marked aria-hidden — ordinal decoration,
 *     and a screen reader already gets the order from the document. It is the
 *     ONE thing keyed to the display index rather than the original one (R6):
 *     after a blank row is dropped the printed sequence must read 01-02-03,
 *     never 01-03. Everything an EDIT touches uses the ORIGINAL index, so
 *     deleting reason 2 can never misdirect a write into reason 3.
 *   · The disc, its ring and the tinted ground. Decorative, no data-field.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED (so none of it reads as an oversight):
 *   · why.image and why.lead. Both are schema-declared and both are used by
 *     sibling templates, but this design's WHY-US has neither a plate nor a lead
 *     paragraph — it is eyebrow, headline, three centred columns. Adding either
 *     would be building a different section than the one that was handed over.
 *     No plate also means no role="img" and no alt anywhere in this file (R9):
 *     there is no upload here to name.
 *   · NO STAR ROW (R2). This section carries no rating field and no review, and
 *     the design draws none here. Stars belong to TESTIMONIALS, and only ever
 *     drawn from a real numeric rating — five stars printed for a customer who
 *     gave no rating is a fabricated claim.
 *   · NO id ON THE SECTION. The page's ids are menu / about / gallery / visit /
 *     faq / main, and the design gives WHY-US none of them; PageBM's nav remaps
 *     a derived '#why' onto '#about'. An id here would create a target the
 *     navigation never points at and a second landing place for the same link.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR HELPER (R7), and this is a decision, not an omission.
 *     The design's WHY-US renders no href of any kind — no button, no link — so
 *     there is nothing to remap and a copied helper would ship as dead code. If
 *     a link is ever added here, copy the helper from a BM sibling with
 *     PAGE_ANCHORS = menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main and
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location,contact -> visit · services,rooms -> menu ·
 *     host,why -> about · work -> gallery · book -> main, passing tel: /
 *     mailto: / http through untouched.
 *   · NO PER-REASON ICON. A drawn pictogram claims WHAT the reason is about; the
 *     owner's words are the only signal this section is allowed to carry. The
 *     disc holds a number, which claims nothing.
 *
 * TRACK COUNT vs ITEM COUNT: the grid is fixed at three tracks because the
 * design fixes three (hint-placeholder-count="3"). An owner who adds a fourth
 * reason gets a second row, not four squeezed columns — the design's proportions
 * survive and no owner content is refused.
 *
 * TWO PIECES OF SECTION GEOMETRY, and why they are handled differently:
 *   · PADDING. This is the one section on the light half with a VISIBLE ground,
 *     so its own padding is drawn rather than merely spacing. The design writes
 *     76px top and bottom; the wrapper's shared rule is clamp(52px,7vw,84px),
 *     which caps 8px higher. The cap is pulled to the drawn 76px here and the
 *     fluid floor is kept, so the band matches the design at full width and
 *     still breathes correctly on a phone.
 *   · MARGIN-TOP. The design writes margin-top:84px on the band because every
 *     unbanded section in it pads TOP only; in the built page the wrapper pads
 *     both ends of every section, so restating it would double the gap. That
 *     bottom-0 rhythm is a WHOLE-PAGE decision that belongs in the wrapper —
 *     one section re-deriving it while fourteen siblings keep theirs is exactly
 *     the local drift that put an earlier template out of step. Flagged, not
 *     done here.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBM passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const why = content.why || {};
const tag = typeof why.tag === 'string' ? why.tag.trim() : '';
// Nothing beyond trim() is done to the headline: it goes out through set:html so
// whatever markup the owner typed reaches the wrapper's italic-walnut em rule.
const headline = typeof why.headline === 'string' ? why.headline.trim() : '';

type Item = { title?: string; body?: string; desc?: string; description?: string };
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(why.items) ? why.items : [];
const titleOf = (it: Item): string => String(it?.title ?? '').trim();
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string => String(it?.body ?? it?.desc ?? it?.description ?? '').trim();

// A reason the owner added but never filled would otherwise spend a ringed disc,
// a numeral and 18px of margin on nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts as
// blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index `i` so every data-field still writes into
// the real array slot.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ i, title: titleOf(it), body: bodyOf(it) }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

// SECTION GATE (R5). Counts the COLUMNS only. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts
// emits why.tag ('Why us') and why.headline ('Why choose X') for every site
// while shipping items: [] — so a gate that counted the tag or the headline
// would be always-true and would ship an eyebrow and a headline over an empty
// tinted band on every first build.
const hasReasons = rows.length > 0;
---
{hasReasons && (
  <section class="tcw">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline) && (
        <div class="tcw-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="why.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 tcw-h2" data-field="why.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}
      <div class="tcw-grid">
        {rows.map((r, d: number) => (
          /* `d` is the DISPLAY index and `r.i` is the ORIGINAL one. The numeral
             is the only thing keyed to `d`, so the printed sequence stays
             unbroken after a blank row is dropped; it is aria-hidden because the
             order is already carried by the document, and it carries no second
             literal aria-hidden anywhere (R9). The stagger tops out at the
             wrapper's last declared delay, so a fourth reason simply reveals
             with the third. */
          <div class="tcw-col reveal" data-delay={d > 0 ? String(Math.min(d, 3)) : undefined}>
            <div class="fig tcw-disc" aria-hidden="true">{String(d + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
            {r.title && <h3 class="tcw-t" data-field={`why.items.${r.i}.title`}>{r.title}</h3>}
            {r.body && <p class="tcw-p" data-field={`why.items.${r.i}.body`}>{r.body}</p>}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── THE TINTED BAND. The design's one soft ground on the light half of the
        page: full-bleed --tint, no rule above or below it — the
        colour change IS the division. Padding is capped at the drawn 76px while
        the wrapper's fluid floor is kept; see the frontmatter for why the
        design's margin-top:84px is deliberately not restated. */
  .tcw {
    background-color: var(--tint);
    padding-block: clamp(52px, 7vw, 76px);
  }

  /* ── the centred head. */
  .tcw-head { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 44px; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .34em tracking, so a
     single unbroken word is unusually wide for its character count — and the
     wrapper's 520 pass only re-sizes .kick, it does not let it break (R8).
     ONLY overflow-wrap is stated: a scoped class out-specifies the wrapper's
     :where(.kick), so restating a font-size here would silently cancel that
     520 pass, and restating the border-bottom would risk losing the signature
     underline outright. */
  .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── the headline. The wrapper's .h2 primitive already carries the design's
        face, clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px), line-height 1.14 and --ink; only what this
        section draws differently is restated — the design's `margin:.26em 0 0`
        (the 44px below belongs to the head, not the heading) and the RELEASED
        measure. The shared primitive caps at 24ch, which on a centred heading
        renders as centred text inside a left-hugging 24ch box; the design gives
        this h2 no max-width at all, so it is released and the .wrap is its
        bound. NO font-size is restated anywhere in this file, at any
        breakpoint, so there is no clamp for a narrower viewport to invert (R8). */
  .tcw-h2 { margin: .26em 0 0; max-width: none; }

  /* ── the three centred columns. Tracks and gap exactly as drawn. */
  .tcw-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 38px;
  }
  /* min-width:0 as well as the minmax() above: a grid item's default min-width
     is auto, so one unbroken word in a title would otherwise widen its track and
     push the whole page sideways (R8). */
  .tcw-col { min-width: 0; text-align: center; }

  /* ── THE DISC. The design's single circle, and the only 50% radius in this
        template — a struck medallion around an ordinal, not a pill around a
        label. It rides the wrapper's .fig primitive for the face, the tabular
        lining figures and line-height 1, and states the ring, the size and the
        22px the design draws. Upright, not italic: this design italicises the
        headline emphasis, not its numerals. */
  .tcw-disc {
    width: 60px; height: 60px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    margin: 0 auto 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-3);
    border-radius: 50%;
    font-size: 22px;
  }

  /* Libre Caslon Text at 400 is the design's weight for this title; it is pinned
     rather than inherited so no heading rule elsewhere can push it onto a face
     this wrapper does not load. LEADING: the design's WHY-US title carries no
     line-height and the design's own stylesheet sets none, so `normal` is
     literally what it draws — restated because PageBM's shared h1-h4 rule is
     1.14, which is the h2's display leading and sets a 23px title tighter than
     it was drawn the moment an owner's reason runs to two lines. Guessing a
     number would be a divergence of its own. */
  .tcw-t {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 23px;
    line-height: normal;
    margin: 0 0 10px; color: var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* PageBM's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` sits at (0,1,1), so this paragraph rule
     is qualified with its parent class to out-specify it — the design's
     paragraph has no bottom margin at all, and its `margin:0 auto` is what
     centres the 34ch measure inside the track. */
  .tcw-col .tcw-p {
    margin: 0 auto; max-width: 34ch;
    font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink-soft);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── 900 · the collapse this family gives a three-track row of TEXT: two
        tracks here, one at 560. Two-up the track is (92vw − 34px) / 2, which is
        ~397px at 900 and ~241px at 561 — so the 34ch measure above binds and
        centres the paragraph over the wide half of this range, and below about
        646px the track is simply narrower than the cap and the paragraph fills
        it. A max-width cannot overflow either way; the tracks are minmax(0,…)
        and every owner string breaks anywhere, so nothing pushes sideways. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .tcw-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 34px; }
    .tcw-head { margin-bottom: 36px; }
  }

  /* ── 560 · one column. minmax(0,...) rather than 1fr: a single track still
        defaults to an auto minimum. The measure cap is RELEASED here, because
        below this width the column is the whole .wrap — 92vw on a phone is
        already a comfortable measure, while a 34ch cap would leave a narrow
        ribbon of text stranded in a wide centred gutter. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .tcw-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 30px; }
    .tcw-col .tcw-p { max-width: none; }
  }

  /* ── 520 · tighten the stack. Nothing here may push the page sideways: the
        overflow-wrap rules and the minmax/min-width tracks cover that, and there
        is no button in this section to give white-space:normal to. The disc
        keeps its drawn 60px — it is a fixed 60px box inside a track that never
        falls below ~290px, so it cannot overflow, and shrinking it would be a
        re-derivation rather than a fit. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .tcw-grid { gap: 26px; }
    .tcw-head { margin-bottom: 28px; }
  }
</style>
